### Summary

The WhatsApp extension calls Baileys' `fetchLatestBaileysVersion()` on **every** socket creation (`extensions/whatsapp/src/session.ts` — `const { version } = await fetchLatestBaileysVersion();`, still the case on current `main`, L259). That helper (baileys `7.0.0-rc13`, `lib/Utils/generics.js`) fetches `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/WhiskeySockets/Baileys/master/src/Defaults/index.ts`, regex-parses line 7, and on **any** failure — network error, non-2xx, timeout, parse miss — **silently** returns the bundled constant `[2, 3000, 1035194821]` with `isLatest: false`. The extension destructures only `{ version }`, so the fallback is invisible in the logs.

That bundled version is already rejected by WhatsApp (see WhiskeySockets/Baileys#2679, #2733, #2376): the handshake is closed with **`status 405` = `client_too_old`**. Net effect: **whenever raw.githubusercontent.com is degraded, every WhatsApp reconnect fails with 405 until GitHub recovers** — a hard runtime dependency on GitHub for a messaging channel that is otherwise healthy.

We hit exactly this during GitHub's incident on 2026-08-17 (https://www.githubstatus.com/incidents/zkxwbgr0cnmx, shortlink https://stspg.io/y1fl26l6wpzr — their 14:04Z update: *"raw repository content downloads are experiencing an approximate 50% error rate"*). Our WhatsApp channel was dead for ~95 min across three windows, all inside the incident, all showing only `Web connection closed during setup (status 405)`. Nothing in the logs mentions the version fetch; root-causing required reading the Baileys source.

### Steps to reproduce

1. Run a linked WhatsApp account on OpenClaw 2026.7.1-2 (baileys 7.0.0-rc13).
2. Make `raw.githubusercontent.com` unreachable or slow from the gateway host (a GitHub incident did it for us; blocking the host in `/etc/hosts` or via a firewall rule reproduces the fallback deterministically).
3. Trigger any reconnect (watchdog recovery, health-monitor restart, or `openclaw gateway restart`).
4. Observe `[whatsapp] Web connection closed during setup (status 405). Retry n/12 …` on every attempt, with no log line explaining why. The channel comes back only when the GitHub fetch succeeds again.

### Expected behavior

- A transient failure to reach GitHub should not take the WhatsApp channel down. The last successfully fetched WA-Web version should be remembered (on disk) and used in preference to the bundled constant, which is known-stale by the time a release ships.
- When the fallback path is taken, say so in the logs (`isLatest === false` plus the swallowed `error` are right there in the return value).
- The fetch should be bounded: during the incident each attempt sat ~1.5–2.5 min before failing (normal is ~0.4 s), which also made the health-monitor's 5 s channel stop time out on every cycle (`[nina] channel stop exceeded 5000ms after abort; continuing shutdown` → restart from Retry 1/12, so backoff never grew).

### Actual behavior

Timeline (AEST = UTC+10; GitHub incident 13:40Z → mitigated 16:36Z):

| window (AEST) | down | what the logs show |
|---|---|---|
| 23:38 → 00:07 | 29 min | 405 on every attempt, 3× health-monitor restart |
| 00:37 → 00:51 | 14 min | 405 on every attempt, 2× restart |
| 01:21 → 02:13 | 52 min | 405 on every attempt, 4× restart + auto-restart |
| 02:43 → | recovered | first clean 4 s recovery after GitHub's 16:36Z "strong signs of recovery" |

Corroboration that it was the fetch and not WhatsApp/the network: `status 405` had never appeared in 11 days of stderr history before this; the upstream `Defaults/index.ts` version had not changed since 2026-07-26 (rules out a WhatsApp min-version bump); the Discord websocket on the same host stayed connected throughout; the moment the fetch got through, the same reconnect code connected in ~4 s. Verified afterwards that the live fetch returns `[2, 3000, 1043857760]` while the bundled fallback is `[2, 3000, 1035194821]`.

